# Git Safety Commits

A previous session accidentally deleted significant sections of `app.rs` through overly broad regex replacements and had no git history to recover from. Prevent this by creating safety commits at key checkpoints.

## When to commit (without being asked)

**Before** any operation that touches more than ~30 lines across multiple files, or that uses multi-line find-and-replace on a large file:
```
git add -A && git commit -m "chore: safety checkpoint before <description>"
```

**After** completing a discrete, working feature or fix — even mid-session:
```
git add -A && git commit -m "<type>: <short description>"
```

## Mandatory checkpoints

| Situation | Action |
|---|---|
| About to do a large refactor (moving/renaming methods, restructuring structs) | Safety commit first |
| About to run a script or shell command that modifies source files | Safety commit first |
| Completed a working build after a multi-step feature | Commit the result |
| Completed a bug fix that compiles cleanly | Commit the result |

## Rules

- Always run `cargo check` before committing Rust code — only commit if it compiles
- Use conventional commit prefixes: `feat:`, `fix:`, `refactor:`, `chore:`
- Never amend commits that have already been pushed
- Do not commit `.env` files, secrets, or build artifacts (`target/`, `portable-build/`)
- When in doubt, commit — a checkpoint commit is always better than losing work
